For one of my branches, Jason did a merge review on the branch and provided solid 
feedback with the five areas as asked. Our team ensured that we went over code quality, 
security, performance, integration compatibility, and documentation with each merge 
review. This format was easy to understand and make changes with, to the point where I 
introduced it into the workplace as I am a software engineer and used it for some merge 
request on our current project.

With the PR, Jason identified some issues, namely build_features() rejecting negative 
workloads but accepting NaN and infinity. He also found some other issues, such as training 
targets being skewed, as well as formatting tips like providing short docstrings on 
functions.

I went ahead and went over the areas that Jason pointed out, making sure to verify and 
agree that the points he was bringing up were good changes to make, and then 
implementing the changes after agreeing. Personally, I use Claude to assist in the decision
making, giving thorough reasoning as to why or why not a recommended change would be 
effective. After conferring, I then went ahead and took care of the changes I needed to make, 
as well as ensuring documentation was updated.

Peer reviews, to me, are incredibly valuable; in the workplace, we use them all the time and 
don’t dare touch merge requests until a team member has reviewed the request. A 
fundamental part of the reviews also helps with familiarity with the codebase, so if you ever 
bring on a new developer, you can throw a smaller merge request towards them so they can 
navigate through the codebase, become familiar with it, and better understand what is 
going on.
